<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Deliberately empty, and that is the decision this file records.

	     com.apple.security.app-sandbox is ABSENT, and cannot be otherwise. This process opens
	     pseudo-terminals and forks the user's agent CLIs into them; a PTY cannot be opened from
	     inside the App Sandbox, which is the same reason Threading itself is unsandboxed —
	     permissions.md: "The sandbox is not a choice that can be revisited." A sandboxed daemon
	     would fail at the first forkpty and be indistinguishable from one that never started.

	     There is no com.apple.developer.* key of any kind. A restricted entitlement on an
	     ad-hoc-signed build is an AMFI kill rather than a warning, and this binary is ad-hoc
	     signed on any machine with no team configured — which is every clean checkout.

	     The containment that does apply is the directory's. The rendezvous and the state
	     directory are 0700 under the user's Application Support, so only this user's processes
	     can reach the socket at all, and no frame carries a token or a capability because none
	     is needed: reaching the socket is the authorization. Nothing here weakens that.

	     Hardened runtime is on (ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME = YES) with no exceptions: the daemon
	     loads no plug-ins, allocates no JIT pages, and disables no library validation. -->
</dict>
</plist>
